# 4. SWARM — Rust backend symbol hygiene

The HashMap experiment originally declared:

```moss
type HashMap:
  ...
```

Moss checking succeeded.

Generated Rust imports:

```rust
use std::collections::{HashMap, VecDeque};
use std::sync::Arc;
```

and emitted the Moss object as:

```rust
struct HashMap { ... }
impl HashMap { ... }
```

causing rustc E0116/name collision.

This is a backend compiler bug.

Moss source names must not be constrained by incidental Rust implementation imports.

## Required invariant

Legal Moss user symbols must not collide with Rust runtime/helper/import names introduced by the backend.

A Moss programmer should be able to write:

```moss
type HashMap:
  value: Int
```

without knowing that the compiler uses Rust `HashMap` internally.

## Preferred implementation

Prefer fully qualified Rust standard-library paths for backend implementation types, for example:

```rust
std::collections::HashMap<...>
std::collections::HashMap::new()
std::collections::VecDeque<...>
std::collections::VecDeque::new()
std::sync::Arc<...>
std::sync::Arc::new(...)
```

and remove broad user-visible imports where practical.

This is preferable to merely aliasing:

```rust
HashMap as __MossHashMap
```

because a Moss user could eventually choose that alias as well unless Moss owns a formally reserved generated namespace.

Do not invent a broad name-mangling framework if fully qualifying backend library symbols solves the proven issue.

## Regression

Create native regressions for at least:

```moss
type HashMap:
  value: Int

type VecDeque:
  value: Int

type Arc:
  value: Int
```

Construct and use each object.

They must:

```text
pass moss check
lower normally
compile under rustc -D warnings
execute correctly
```

Also ensure built-in:

```text
Map
Queue
domain runtime Arc usage
```

still lower successfully.

## Restore the HashMap experiment name

After this compiler bug is fixed, rename the dogfooding object back from:

```moss
IntHashMap
```

to:

```moss
HashMap
```

unless there is an independent Moss-language naming conflict.

This demonstrates the bug is genuinely fixed rather than merely hidden.

Its factory may remain:

```moss
hash_map(...)
```

No need to rename built-in Moss `Map`.

